\section{What a selection must preserve}

In \emph{Instruction Selection: Principles, Methods, and Applications}, Gabriel Hjort Blindell follows one function through three backend tasks: first choose instructions, then decide when they can execute, and finally assign their operands to machine registers (\cite[chapter~1, section~1.1]{blindell2016instruction}). These boundaries describe responsibilities rather than independent problems. A selected instruction can require two operands to share a register, which gives the allocator a constraint it did not have before. A selected sequence can also impose an order the scheduler cannot remove.

The input to a selector has a contract. For a block region, specify the values available at entry, the values demanded at exit, and any effects the surrounding program can observe. In a pure arithmetic region this can be just a mapping between live values, with no traps or memory events. In a region that may fault or communicate with memory, comparing only returned data is insufficient: the target must preserve the relevant effects and the conditions under which control continues. A selected instruction need not resemble the IR operation syntactically. A single instruction can implement several IR operations; a single IR operation can also need several instructions.

\section{Find candidates before comparing them}

Blindell distinguishes \emph{pattern matching}, which finds the instructions applicable to parts of the IR, from \emph{pattern selection}, which decides on a combination of candidates (\cite[chapter~1, section~1.1.4]{blindell2016instruction}). For a given input, a candidate should identify the part it implements, the inputs and outputs at its boundary, and any remaining obligations. A structural match can name a promising form without settling those obligations: the opcode might require a target feature, a particular operand class, or a copy to satisfy a register tie. If an obligation cannot be met, the candidate is unavailable; increasing its estimated cost does not make it legal.

Consider a target with flag-free instructions for eight-bit registers. The region computes a mask $r=\neg(x\mathbin{\oplus}y)$, with complementation limited to eight bits. The target can use a two-byte \texttt{XOR3} with a separate destination and a one-byte in-place \texttt{INV}. It also offers a two-byte \texttt{XNOR2 a,b} that overwrites \texttt{a} with the complemented XOR of the two old operands. The remaining instruction is a two-byte \texttt{COPY}. Assume enough registers for the described plans and no placement constraints except the destination ties; these forms have no other side effects. Both inputs are needed inside the region, but the continuation dictates whether either value may be overwritten.

