\section{Price a destructive match at the boundary}

Suppose $x$ is initially in \texttt{A}, $y$ in \texttt{B}, and \texttt{C} is available. Compare the costs of complete plans, including any copy needed to keep an input live. Since XOR is commutative, the tied destination can use either input when that input dies. With both inputs live, merely swapping them cannot solve the problem.

\begin{verbatim}
Inputs: A = 0xD6, B = 0x3C.  Result required at exit.
XOR3 C,A,B; INV C       # 2 + 1 bytes; preserve both inputs
    C: 0xEA -> 0x15
XNOR2 A,B               # 2 bytes; valid if old A is dead
    A: 0xD6 -> 0x15
COPY C,A; XNOR2 C,B     # 2 + 2 bytes; pay for the tie
    C: 0xD6 -> 0x15
\end{verbatim}

The calculation uses \texttt{0xD6} XOR \texttt{0x3C} equal to \texttt{0xEA}; the eight-bit complement is \texttt{0x15}. In the case where $x$ dies, choose the two-byte plan over the three-byte plan. With both inputs live, choose the three-byte plan over the four-byte plan. In particular, a continuation that returns $x$ as well as $r$ is a negative test for the bare destructive match: it would return \texttt{0x15} in place of \texttt{0xD6}. The invariant is that every value demanded at the boundary, including a value that merely passes through the region, is available in its recorded location. A symbolic value match for $r$ alone does not establish that invariant.

In a virtual-register backend, a destructive form can be represented by a tie between an input and an output, not by assigning register \texttt{A} immediately. Copies the selector expects to disappear may survive allocation; conversely, a dying source may let the allocator satisfy a tie without an emitted copy. Record which bytes are part of the selection and which depend on a later placement decision.

