Blindell's definition of optimal selection fixes an instruction set and minimizes the sum of instruction costs among implementations. He makes two qualifications: the supported forms and IR representation limit what can be found, and costs may not compose additively when scheduling and allocation matter (\cite[chapter~1, section~1.2.2]{blindell2016instruction}). In the example, the claimed minima are over the displayed plans and the stated placements, measured in bytes before any other backend changes. A smaller plan may slow execution, and abundant scratch registers in the example do not justify assuming the same availability in a larger block.

To compare selectors, hold target features and the input representation fixed and state which cost is optimized. Measure final bytes or execution separately from the selector's predicted score. Also compare against a legal baseline so that a failure to select does not masquerade as a cheap result. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-263} starts with a baseline that handles one normalized operation at a time. Its speed comes from limiting the decision, not from solving the unrestricted optimization problem.
